WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:14.020
So I kept on rewriting the title.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.030
This is not a bad title for this class.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:22.190
Another way of looking at it is instead
of trying to move forward from here,

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:26.595
where everything is confusing, and
the present looks like reality.

00:00:26.595 --> 00:00:29.790
We want to go out to the future and
bring it back.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.835
And we talked about some process and
methods,

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:40.225
today we're going to talk about
a few of the gazillion things.

00:00:40.225 --> 00:00:45.789
Of course I didn't get done last
week which made this week even more.

00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:50.037
And finally, I decided **** it
I'm going to do both of these

00:00:50.037 --> 00:00:55.020
talks again without an audience to
do them to their natural length.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.479
Because the destiny for
this is being online and

00:00:58.479 --> 00:01:02.380
online people are not
confined to 50 minute hours.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:05.315
I'm sure Sam was all for that, great.

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:10.235
So again, here's my email address.

00:01:10.235 --> 00:01:14.847
Now, of all the people who are tuned
into this talk and in the classroom,

00:01:14.847 --> 00:01:16.139
I got three emails.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.640
One of which was interesting.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.630
That means that either or

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.820
both of my talk isn't interesting,
or you're not interesting, or both.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.460
So try and, at least,
disabuse me of the latter.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:41.035
Okay, to start off with,
here's Leonardo, pretty smart guy.

00:01:41.035 --> 00:01:45.516
Imagine if you were born
with twice Leonardo's IQ.

00:01:45.516 --> 00:01:47.746
Think of how easy it would
be to get into Stanford.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.787
Maybe you do have twice Leonardo's IQ.

00:01:51.787 --> 00:01:56.600
But suppose you were born with
twice Leonardo's IQ in 10,000 BC.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:57.665
How far are you going to get?

00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:01.150
Zero, zip, right?

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.790
Before they burn you at the stake,
or whatever they did back then.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:12.950
Yeah, so here's a guy with nowhere
near Leonardo's IQ, Henry Ford.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:19.707
And whereas Leonardo could
not invent a single motor for

00:02:19.707 --> 00:02:23.171
any of his vehicles, think about that.

00:02:23.171 --> 00:02:24.710
So he was smart, but he wasn't that smart.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:31.423
Henry Ford was able to put together
processes that allowed him to make

00:02:31.423 --> 00:02:37.092
millions and millions of cars for
about, sell them for $800 a piece.

00:02:37.092 --> 00:02:41.292
And the difference between the two was
that Ford was born into the right century,

00:02:41.292 --> 00:02:43.719
and Leonardo was born
into the wrong century.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:50.250
And so the knowledge that had
accumulated in the 19th century made

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.970
much more difference than
any level of smartness.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.450
And where did that knowledge come from,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:01.461
it came might as well pick Newton
if you're going to pick anybody.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.170
It came because the context was
changed from the Middle Ages and

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:11.640
the Renaissance to something completely
different in the 17th century.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.580
And Newton made the biggest
change of anybody.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:19.470
And so we can call that
change a change in outlook.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:24.515
We can call it a change in context, world
view, point of view, whatever you want.

00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:30.460
So when I'm numbering rules for you I
decided to go back and start with zero.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:35.120
So the zeroth rule here,
probably the most important one is

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:40.450
to make progress here,
let me move this guy off.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.690
Context or
point of view is worth 80 IQ points.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:53.140
If you've got the context of science and
math, you're a gazillion of times smarter,

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.130
effectively, than the smartest
people of antiquity like Archimedes.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:02.222
A person who learns calculus
with an IQ of 110 or

00:04:02.222 --> 00:04:07.788
120 can do things that the greatest
geniuses of the past can't do.

00:04:07.788 --> 00:04:11.948
Okay, so we can sum that up by saying

00:04:11.948 --> 00:04:16.800
knowledge is silver, context is gold.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.451
And, IQ is just lead.

00:04:19.451 --> 00:04:21.802
It's a lead weight on you.

00:04:21.802 --> 00:04:26.863
Biggest problem with most people
who are at your level and

00:04:26.863 --> 00:04:30.448
going to the next level is you're always

00:04:30.448 --> 00:04:35.313
the smartest people in your classroom or
room at home.

00:04:35.313 --> 00:04:40.700
But that only means you're the smartest
person amongst 30 or a 100.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:45.190
And if you happened to be in a room that
had the 100 smartest people in the world.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:48.480
And you were the smartest
person in that room,

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.670
you're still not as smart as
the other 99 working together.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.130
So being clever doesn't do it.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.460
And this is the simplest
snare is the IQ snare.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.180
Tony Hoare, really great guy,
was here at Stanford for

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.520
awhile Turing winner, said debugging
is harder than programming so

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.150
don't use all your cleverness
to write the program.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.400
Anybody recognize this?

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.092
Anybody write programs here?

00:05:16.092 --> 00:05:17.480
Let me see hands.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:18.910
Yeah, so

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:22.720
people are so clever writing the program
they forget that debugging is harder.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.690
And that's true about anything.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:28.066
So that's principle number one,
we had zero.

00:05:28.066 --> 00:05:30.660
And then here's a summary
of what I showed you.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:36.262
And the number 2 one at the top there
is that the goodness of the results

00:05:36.262 --> 00:05:39.957
correlates most strongly with
the goodness of the funders.

00:05:39.957 --> 00:05:43.121
This is the simplest way
of summing up the past.

00:05:43.121 --> 00:05:47.461
because you have to realize the difference
in the past wasn't that the people were

00:05:47.461 --> 00:05:51.420
smarter back then, we weren't
smarter than the smart people today.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:55.210
Every generation has really smart people.

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:59.180
The difference in whether
you get enormous results or

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:04.460
not, is in this context idea and
the funders that support it.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:07.210
One of the ways of thinking about them is,

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:12.300
as opposed to getting gold medals like a
bunch of us have gotten in our sixties and

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:15.940
seventies, 40 years
after you did the deeds.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.572
Good funders give out the gold
models ahead of time.

00:06:18.572 --> 00:06:22.105
Knowing that 70% of them
are going to turn to lead,

00:06:22.105 --> 00:06:27.240
because the funders give it out
before you've gotten the results.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.765
So this will be a slide
that you can use later on,

00:06:30.765 --> 00:06:33.688
it's basically what I said last time.

00:06:33.688 --> 00:06:37.641
Now last time we looked at four systems.

00:06:37.641 --> 00:06:41.189
Sketchpad, we didn't really look
at the Engelbart system, but

00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:43.250
it's easy to find out about online.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:47.150
We looked at the first really
good gesture system, GRAIL.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.260
And we saw that Ivan Sutherland, again,

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:55.220
invented VR a long,
long before it ever came to use.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:00.179
And I really rushed past
personal computers.

00:07:00.179 --> 00:07:04.268
The first thing that was kind
of like a personal computer,

00:07:04.268 --> 00:07:09.286
it was kind of cute, was
Henry Husky's Bend XG 15 in the mid-50s.

00:07:09.286 --> 00:07:11.321
So this was really early.

00:07:11.321 --> 00:07:16.850
Harry just left us a few
years over the age of 100.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:18.750
Really wonderful guy.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:23.410
Here's my vote for the first real personal
computer called the LINC by Wes Clark.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.917
Wes Clark also did the building size
computer that Sketchpad was done on.

00:07:27.917 --> 00:07:30.620
So he liked to work both large and small.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.745
Here's one I did with Ed Cheadle.

00:07:34.919 --> 00:07:35.980
Looks kind of modern.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.810
And an idea I had in 1968,

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:46.750
which struck me really deeply,
not because it was a tablet, but

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:48.980
because it was a thing
that children needed.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:55.290
And I'd learned from Seymour Papert what
children could actually do with computers.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:08:00.070
And that seized me in a romantic fashion.

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:02.741
And we looked at this idea of better and

00:08:02.741 --> 00:08:06.330
perfect are the enemy Of
what is actually needed.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:09.300
In other words, you have to draw
thresholds in order to know where you

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.590
are or else you're just
measuring local variations.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:14.070
You don't know whether they're good or
bad.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.740
And the last thing we did on Tuesday
was looking at this process of

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:24.160
taking a glimmer of an idea out 30 or
more years,

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.390
to see if it makes more sense 30
years from now than it seems to now.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:31.700
And if it does, you bring it back.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:38.290
Simulate it by making super computers
that allow you to do the software.

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:40.360
And then five or eight years later,

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.810
you've got something that
will revolutionize the world.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:48.050
This is where the trillions
rather than billions

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.830
comes in because you create an industry.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:52.380
And last time,

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:58.100
I didn't get a chance to show you kind of
just one of the examples of what PARC did.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.770
So I thought I'd do it today.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:08.140
And this is a self-portrait, because
now I have extracted stuff from a demo.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:14.957
But this is a system we brought back
to life from 1978, so you get a chance.

00:09:14.957 --> 00:09:16.820
And this is a self-portrait.

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:21.823
So this is the successor to
the Alto called the Dorado.

00:09:21.823 --> 00:09:29.645
So it had now bigger than XVGA screen
rather than the portrait model.

00:09:29.645 --> 00:09:34.964
And this dithering technique was
invented at the same time in

00:09:34.964 --> 00:09:40.500
the 70s by Bob Floyd and Steinberg,
and partly done at PARC.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:45.950
So this is what a typical image on only
a few computers in the world looked like,

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.730
back then, one bit per point.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.860
And where do we get this system from?

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:55.890
Well, here's the Xerox dump.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:10:01.350
And if you closely there, there's a disk
pack, actually we got Xerox through out.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:05.240
Most of the disk packs of the work
that was done at Xerox PARC,

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.000
few of them got rescued.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.580
Here it is, few hundred files on there.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:17.116
One of them happened to be most
of a system that we did in 1978.

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:21.170
And it's of partial interest
because the next year,

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:27.250
it was the stuff that Steve Jobs got
shown doing his famous visit to.

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:28.960
So you have a little flavor here.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.450
Now what we did is we
brought this back to life.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.364
And it was relatively easy because
the system is called Smalltalk.

00:10:37.364 --> 00:10:45.150
Smalltalk-78 is a pure object system,
and so it resembles the internet.

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:50.090
This is an internet self-portrait and it's
just as a good a self-portrait, Smalltalk.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:53.930
Because the idea was to have one-to-one

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:58.630
correspondence between the idea
of little logical computers.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:04.580
Which is what we called objects
that could send messages around and

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.710
make an entire system.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:13.600
And so it's a little different than from
what you think object-oriented programming

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:14.210
is today.

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:20.710
One of the consequences is that there
are no actual applications because

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:24.810
you can mix and
match objects as you choose.

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.250
Here we see something
that is vaguely familiar.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:30.483
I thought I turned that off.

00:11:30.483 --> 00:11:34.388
Okay, so
this is a typical screen back then, and

00:11:34.388 --> 00:11:39.860
it ran on the Alto there,
the portable note taker, and the Dorado.

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:44.250
Here's a bitmap painting I did 45 years
ago that we found and stuck in here.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:51.810
See the user interface is with the object
itself, and here I'm marking it up.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:57.680
And it has a character recognizer,
gesture recognizer like GRAIL did.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:00.180
There it's recognizing I want an arrow and
making one.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.880
The overlapping windows, the icons.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.390
What would be called Rich Text today.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:14.450
And you can see the gesture recognizer
is being used to tell it how to justify.

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:17.380
And this system is organized
in terms of projects.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:22.718
So each project you can think of in
today's terms as a separate desktop,

00:12:22.718 --> 00:12:24.062
they persist over time.

00:12:24.062 --> 00:12:27.801
Now think about this compared
to the user interface today,

00:12:27.801 --> 00:12:33.520
where you use an application that doesn't
have every feature that you want.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:38.220
And you start and stop applications,
and try and cut and paste between them.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:43.350
Here what you have is just working spaces,
an unlimited desk for

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:44.670
every project that you have.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:51.540
You can have as many, they persist over
time because this is a workflow idea.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.928
We couldn't get Apple to adopt this idea.

00:12:55.928 --> 00:13:00.070
And so these are kind of
thumbnails of each one of them.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.960
We can go into one of them.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:06.780
Here's a little thing done
by a 13-year old girl,

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:11.420
writing an article for
Creative Computing back in 1975.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:17.050
And so you can see it's sort
of like desktop publishing.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:22.270
But in fact,
any object can be used in here.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:27.230
And so for example, if we look here,
she's telling about how she

00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:32.500
used the program and
how she's typing in there.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:36.961
She's typing in code, and it's being
executed to with the box and turn the box,

00:13:36.961 --> 00:13:37.756
and so forth.

00:13:37.756 --> 00:13:40.010
All right, get the idea?

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:44.690
So, it'd be nice if Wikipedia had
even thought about this, right?

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:50.170
Because imagine,
you go to the article on logo, guess what?

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:52.031
You can't execute logo code in there.

00:13:52.031 --> 00:13:53.740
Why?
It's on a computer.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.223
What didn't they get about computing?

00:13:56.223 --> 00:13:57.420
Well, pretty much everything.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:03.080
If you look at it closely,
you'll see the web design and

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:06.130
the web browser are two of the worst
things ever done in computing.

00:14:07.188 --> 00:14:10.360
Because they failed to take account of the
fact that you're actually on a computer.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:16.960
Okay, another example here
is an animation system.

00:14:18.912 --> 00:14:21.710
You notice that ball is
not squashing there.

00:14:24.554 --> 00:14:27.500
And it would look better if
the ball squashed when it hit.

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:32.480
So if this were normal system and
it didn't have that feature, we'd be done.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:36.600
But we can relate objects to each other.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:40.970
So here we're single stepping to get
to that frame that we'd like to change.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:46.460
We're looking at the interface
of this bouncing guy, and

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:49.450
we're seeing, yeah,
there's a thing called current frame.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:51.640
That's what this thing is.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.220
And here's our painting window.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.010
And let's look at its interface.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:03.600
And here's something that allows
me to set the picture in there.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.780
And I want to connect
the two windows together, so

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:08.770
I use the gesture recognizer
to draw a line between them.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:13.170
It gives me a little place to
explain how I'd like to relate them.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:16.870
And I save all the painter's picture,

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:22.040
wants to be the bouncing
animation's current frame.

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:27.420
Okay, everybody get what's going on here?

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.090
So I say, okay, go ahead and do that, and

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:31.720
now it's latched onto
that thing that's there.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:37.780
Now we can start the animation going
again, but while it's going, I can paint.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:43.195
Look carefully and you'll see that the
thing is being painted while it's running.

00:15:43.195 --> 00:15:44.480
Won't you like to be
able to do that today?

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:47.700
And just think about, why not?

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:52.713
This is like sketch pad, right?

00:15:52.713 --> 00:15:53.800
Except a few years later.

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:56.681
Yeah, this is completely natural.

00:15:56.681 --> 00:16:02.470
It's what you get very, very easily
if you just have unlimited objects.

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:07.440
So in this Particular thing gives you
automatically something much more

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:11.240
powerful in PowerPoint,
because all you have to do is sequence

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:13.650
these things called projects for
the desktops.

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:14.560
That's what I'm doing here.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:17.710
I'm just moving from one to another.

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:21.220
So that's an example of
what happened at Parc,

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:25.610
and if we go back to the screen
of the various inventions there.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:29.810
We can look at this now I'm
answering Sam's question.

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:37.560
Well how much of the alto idea
actually made it out of Xerox parc.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:38.190
Well about half.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:42.860
We'll see that see what
the other half is in a second.

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:43.770
How about the GUI?

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:47.910
Well about 70%,
because you notice the GUI didn't

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:53.050
come out as something that has
this unlimited desktop idea.

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:58.850
Even today, 30 some odd years after
this nobody has put it in there.

00:16:58.850 --> 00:17:00.360
It's ridiculous.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:08.140
But it has to do with people wanting to
have application and sell applications.

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:11.459
Even though most applications won't
do all the things that you need.

