00:52:08.660 --> 00:52:13.560
The talk on Tuesday, maybe a little
thicker today, but the whole impetus

00:52:13.560 --> 00:52:21.620
behind the ARPA research and
inventions of these things.

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:26.559
And particularly,
people like Engelbart was to try

00:52:26.559 --> 00:52:30.520
to invent new tools and

00:52:30.520 --> 00:52:34.440
new media for humanity to get
itself out of its problems.

00:52:35.700 --> 00:52:40.770
And Engelbart, for example,
said almost everything important

00:52:40.770 --> 00:52:46.440
that has consequence in the adult world
is done by adults working together.

00:52:47.780 --> 00:52:50.850
And so this is why his system
was collaborative from the,

00:52:50.850 --> 00:52:52.440
so here's an interesting thing.

00:52:52.440 --> 00:52:56.750
So here's a Mac, some people have Linux,
some people have Windows on it.

00:52:58.680 --> 00:53:00.040
Here's the interesting thing.

00:53:00.040 --> 00:53:05.130
Not a single one of the main operating
systems today has built into it

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:09.950
the thing that Englebart showed in 1968,
which is the intrinsic

00:53:09.950 --> 00:53:15.330
ability to share any content that
you're looking at with anybody else,

00:53:15.330 --> 00:53:20.800
to the point of allowing them to interact
with it and to talk back and forth, right?

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:21.837
Skype, it doesn't do it.

00:53:24.458 --> 00:53:26.938
So this a thing that is at
an operating system level.

00:53:26.938 --> 00:53:28.586
It's not a trivial thing to do.

00:53:28.586 --> 00:53:33.850
It is possible to do, but not a single
operating system use today does it.

00:53:33.850 --> 00:53:40.970
And so the ability to actually work
collaboratively on content is been held

00:53:40.970 --> 00:53:47.306
back by notions of operating
systems that go back pre-1965.

00:53:47.306 --> 00:53:53.040
All three operating systems we have today
that are the main ones are old, old ideas.

00:53:53.040 --> 00:53:56.067
They don't even get to where Park was,

00:53:56.067 --> 00:54:00.764
the notion of how processes can
coordinate with each other.

00:54:00.764 --> 00:54:04.371
And there are many, many other of
these things, because these operating

00:54:04.371 --> 00:54:08.910
systems are rather similar to each other,
and because they're pervasive.

00:54:08.910 --> 00:54:14.380
Unless you use your reality kit, you're
going to think that they're normal and,

00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:17.090
therefore, that's the way
things should be.

00:54:17.090 --> 00:54:20.948
Many people, in computing,
have a misplaced notion of

00:54:20.948 --> 00:54:25.560
Darwinian process, Like most people.

00:54:26.620 --> 00:54:30.160
Most people think Darwinian
processes optimize.

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:31.640
That is absolutely not the truth.

00:54:32.822 --> 00:54:36.010
One of my degrees in molecular biology,
and I can tell you,

00:54:36.010 --> 00:54:39.465
any biologist would say,
they're absolutely not optimized.

00:54:39.465 --> 00:54:43.652
The whole point of Darwinian processes is

00:54:43.652 --> 00:54:48.086
to fit into some niche
in some environment.

00:54:48.086 --> 00:54:52.192
And if that environment isn't the right
kind of environment, the processes of

00:54:52.192 --> 00:54:55.819
evolution is not going to give you
something that's very interesting.

00:54:56.870 --> 00:54:58.750
That is the way it works.

00:54:58.750 --> 00:55:04.300
And as computing gets less and
less interesting, its way of accepting and

00:55:04.300 --> 00:55:07.470
rejecting things gets more and
more mundane.

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:12.580
So this is why some of these early
systems, like Sam looks at sketchpads and

00:55:12.580 --> 00:55:14.030
said, why aren't they doing it today?

00:55:14.030 --> 00:55:16.530
Well, because nobody even thinks
about that that's important.

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:25.224
Nobody even thinks of doing
Wiziwig on the web, on web media.

00:55:25.224 --> 00:55:29.388
I just typed in some answers to Cora, and

00:55:29.388 --> 00:55:33.340
I was in a regime that was pre-70s.

00:55:33.340 --> 00:55:36.480
I was typing into a little window.

00:55:36.480 --> 00:55:40.020
I couldn't see what it was going to
look like until I clicked the button.

00:55:40.020 --> 00:55:41.135
Come on, this is ****.

00:55:41.135 --> 00:55:43.249
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:55:43.249 --> 00:55:48.445
nobody is protesting except old fogies
like me, because I know it can be better.

00:55:48.445 --> 00:55:51.240
You need to find out that it can
be better, that is your job.

00:55:52.750 --> 00:55:55.540
Your job is not to agree with me,
your job is to wake up.

00:55:57.250 --> 00:56:00.230
Find ways of criticizing
the stuff that seems normal.

00:56:00.230 --> 00:56:02.120
That is the only way out of the soup.

00:56:04.100 --> 00:56:07.213
You have to go against your
genetic impulses to try and

00:56:07.213 --> 00:56:11.518
